\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

In recent years, the rapid development of large-scale machine learning models has greatly improved the performance of various tasks. However, such models often require a large amount of training data and computational resources, which limits their practical applications in many scenarios. To address this issue, transfer learning has emerged as an effective approach to leverage knowledge from pre-trained models for downstream tasks with limited labeled data \citep{pan2010survey}. Specifically, transfer learning aims to adapt a pre-trained model to a new task by fine-tuning the model parameters on the target dataset. 

In the context of computer vision, the most popular approach is to use pre-trained convolutional neural networks (CNNs) as feature extractors and then train a linear classifier on top of the extracted features \citep{simonyan2014very, he2016deep, zhang2018shufflenet, liu2019mixnet, huang2017densely, liu2019efficientnet, tan2019mnasnet, radosavovic2020deit}. This method has been widely adopted due to its simplicity and effectiveness. However, it is generally observed that the performance of the linear classifier heavily depends on the quality of the extracted features, which can be suboptimal when the pre-trained model is not well-aligned with the target task. To improve the performance, one can further fine-tune the entire model on the target dataset, but this often leads to overfitting, especially when the target dataset is small.

To mitigate the above issues, various methods have been proposed to enhance the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained model. For example, \citet{liu2021rethinking} propose to retrain the last few layers of the pre-trained model on the target dataset. \citet{wang2021feature} propose to replace the pre-trained feature extractor with a new backbone network. \citet{chen2022towards} propose to learn a task-specific representation by jointly optimizing the feature extractor and the linear classifier. However, these methods still rely on a fixed pre-trained model and may not fully exploit the potential of the target dataset.

To address these limitations, we propose a novel approach called \textbf{F}eature \textbf{E}xtension \textbf{T}ransfer \textbf{L}earning (\textbf{FE-Trans}) framework. Our key insight is that the pre-trained model can be further enhanced by incorporating the target dataset into the training process, rather than treating it as a fixed feature extractor. Specifically, we introduce a set of task-specific feature extension modules (FEMs) to the pre-trained model, which are trained to capture the unique characteristics of the target dataset. These FEMs are designed to be lightweight and computationally efficient, making them suitable for deployment in resource-constrained environments. By integrating the target dataset into the training process, FE-Trans can effectively leverage the rich information contained in the dataset to improve the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained model.

The main contributions of our work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose a novel feature extension transfer learning framework, which incorporates the target dataset into the training process to enhance the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained model.
    \item We design a set of lightweight and computationally efficient feature extension modules to capture the unique characteristics of the target dataset.
    \item We conduct extensive experiments on various benchmark datasets and demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach in improving the performance of the pre-trained model on downstream tasks.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Transfer Learning in Computer Vision}
Transfer learning has become a standard practice in computer vision, where pre-trained models are used as feature extractors for downstream tasks. The most common approach is to fine-tune the pre-trained model on the target dataset, either by training a linear classifier on top of the extracted features or by fine-tuning the entire model \citep{pan2010survey, szegedy2015going, he2016deep, chen2020improved, zhang2020rethinking, xie2020rethinking, wang2021feature, zhang2021improved, liu2021rethinking, liu2021revisiting, chen2022towards, zhao2022few, zhao2022learning, yang2022transfer}. However, these approaches often suffer from overfitting when the target dataset is small, and the performance of the linear classifier is highly dependent on the quality of the extracted features.

\paragraph{Feature Extension Modules}
To improve the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained model, various feature extension modules have been proposed. For example, \citet{liu2021rethinking} propose to retrain the last few layers of the pre-trained model on the target dataset. \citet{wang2021feature} propose to replace the pre-trained feature extractor with a new backbone network. \citet{chen2022towards} propose to learn a task-specific representation by jointly optimizing the feature extractor and the linear classifier. However, these methods still rely on a fixed pre-trained model and may not fully exploit the potential of the target dataset.

\paragraph{Data-Driven Transfer Learning}
To address the limitations of existing transfer learning methods, some works have proposed to incorporate the target dataset into the training process. For example, \citet{zhang2020rethinking} propose to train a linear classifier on the concatenation of the pre-trained features and the target dataset. \citet{zhao2022few} propose to use a data-driven approach to select the most informative samples from the target dataset for fine-tuning. \citet{yang2022transfer} propose to learn a task-specific representation by jointly optimizing the feature extractor and the target dataset. However, these methods still rely on a fixed pre-trained model and may not fully exploit the potential of the target dataset.

\paragraph{Our Approach}
In contrast to the above methods, we propose a novel feature extension transfer learning framework, which incorporates the target dataset into the training process to enhance the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained model. Specifically, we introduce a set of task-specific feature extension modules (FEMs) to the pre-trained model, which are trained to capture the unique characteristics of the target dataset. These FEMs are designed to be lightweight and computationally efficient, making them suitable for deployment in resource-constrained environments. By integrating the target dataset into the training process, FE-Trans can effectively leverage the rich information contained in the dataset to improve the feature extraction capability of the pre-trained model.

