```
class App extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {value: ''};
    this.handleChange = this.handleChange.bind(this);
    this.handleSubmit = this.handleSubmit.bind(this);
  }

  handleChange(event) {
    this.setState({value: event.target.value});
  }

  handleSubmit(event) {
    console.log('value is ' + this.state.value);
    event.preventDefault();
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <form onSubmit={this.handleSubmit}>
          <label>
            Name:
            <input type="text" value={this.state.value} onChange={this.handleChange} />
          </label>
          <input type="submit" value="Submit" />
        </form>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

```

But when I run this code, the submit button doesn't do anything. What am I doing wrong?
The issue you're encountering is due to the fact that by default, the `<form>` element's `onSubmit` event will trigger a page reload if no action is specified for the form. To prevent this behavior, you need to explicitly call `event.preventDefault()` within your `handleSubmit` method to stop the default form submission action.

Here's your corrected code:

```jsx
import React from 'react';

class App extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { value: '' };
    this.handleChange = this.handleChange.bind(this);
    this.handleSubmit = this.handleSubmit.bind(this);
  }

  handleChange(event) {
    this.setState({ value: event.target.value });
  }

  handleSubmit(event) {
    console.log('value is ' + this.state.value);
    event.preventDefault(); // Prevent the default form submission
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <form onSubmit={this.handleSubmit}>
          <label>
            Name:
            <input type="text" value={this.state.value} onChange={this.handleChange} />
          </label>
          <input type="submit" value="Submit" />
        </form>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default App;
```

### Explanation:
- **`event.preventDefault()`**: This method stops the default action of the event (in this case, the form submission), which prevents the page from reloading.
- **Binding Methods**: The `this` context needs to be correctly bound in class methods. You can use arrow functions or bind them in the constructor as shown above.

This should work as expected, logging the input value to the console when the submit button is clicked.

