// Function to find the largest prime factor of a given integer n.
long findLargestPrimeFactor(long n) {
    long maxPrime = -1;

    // Divide n by 2 to remove all even factors
    while (n % 2 == 0) {
        maxPrime = 2;
        n /= 2;
    }

    // Now n must be odd, start checking from 3
    for (long i = 3; i <= std::sqrt(n); i += 2) {
        // While i divides n, print i and divide n
        while (n % i == 0) {
            maxPrime = i;
            n = n / i;
        }
    }

    // This condition is to check if n is a prime number greater than 2
    if (n > 2) {
        maxPrime = n;
    }

    return maxPrime;
}

// Main function to test the findLargestPrimeFactor function.
int main() {
    std::cout << "Largest prime factor of 13195: " << findLargestPrimeFactor(13195) << std::endl; // Should output 29
    std::cout << "Largest prime factor of 600851475143: " << findLargestPrimeFactor(600851475143) << std::endl; // Should output 6857

    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a helper function `isPrime` to check if a number is prime. The main function `findLargestPrimeFactor` finds the largest prime factor by first removing all factors of 2 (the only even prime), then iterating through odd numbers up to the square root of the remaining number. If after this process the remaining number is greater than 2, it is a prime number and thus the largest prime factor. The `main` function tests the solution with two examples.

