Introduction to LINQ in C#
Pages: 1, 2, 3

LINQ to SQL

Now that you've learned how to use LINQ to query and manipulate data, let's take a look at LINQ to SQL. LINQ to SQL is a data-access technology for .NET developers. It allows you to work with relational data as strongly typed objects in your .NET applications. It also includes a powerful object-relational mapping (ORM) engine.

The basic idea behind LINQ to SQL is that it allows you to work with the database using a programming language, rather than a query language such as T-SQL. This allows you to write code that can be compiled and debugged using all of the tools that you're already familiar with, rather than having to learn and use a separate query language.

To get started, you need to create a new LINQ to SQL class. You do this by adding a new item to your project, and then choosing "LINQ to SQL Classes" from the list of templates. You will then be prompted to add a connection to a database, and once you have done so, you can start dragging tables into your new class file.

For example, here I've added a connection to the AdventureWorks sample database, and dragged the Product table into my class file:

Adding a LINQ to SQL class

When you drag a table into the designer, LINQ to SQL creates a class for each table, which represents a single row in the table. It also creates a DataContext class, which represents a connection to the database.

Each class created by the LINQ to SQL designer has a number of properties, which represent columns in the table. For example, here are the properties on the Product class:

public partial class Product : INotifyPropertyChanging, 
    INotifyPropertyChanged {
        private int _ProductID;
        private string _Name;
        private string _ProductNumber;
        private string _Color;
        private bool _SafetyStockLevelIsNull;
        private short? _SafetyStockLevel;
        // ... etc ...

As you can see, there is a property for each column in the table. The properties that represent nullable columns (such as SafetyStockLevel) are nullable types, while the other columns are represented as non-nullable types. The properties that represent non-nullable columns also have a boolean IsNull property, which you can use to determine if the column contains a null value.

The DataContext class is used to manage the connection to the database, and it also provides methods for querying and manipulating the data. Here's an example of a simple query that uses the DataContext:

var query = db.Products
    .Where(p => p.Color == "Blue");

The query variable now contains a sequence of products that match the query criteria. You can iterate through the results like this:

foreach (var product in query) {
    Console.WriteLine("{0} - {1}", product.ProductID, product.Name);

If you want to insert or update records in the database, you can do so using the InsertOnSubmit and UpdateOnSubmit methods:

// Create a new product
var product = new Product() {
    Name = "T-shirt",
    Color = "Red",
    ListPrice = 19.95m,
    Size = "M"

// Add the new product to the database


// Update an existing product
var existingProduct = db.Products.First(p => p.ProductID == 488);
existingProduct.Name = "T-shirt, XL";

// Update the changes in the database

Notice that when you call InsertOnSubmit or UpdateOnSubmit, the changes aren't actually made to the database until you call SubmitChanges. This means that you can use the same object for multiple inserts or updates, and you don't have to worry about keeping track of which changes have been made.

You can also use LINQ to SQL to perform more complex queries. For example, here's a query that finds all of the products that were discontinued after 2005:

var query = from p in db.Products
            where p.DiscontinuedDate > new DateTime(2005, 1, 1)
            select p;

And here's a query that finds the names of all of the products that contain the word "t-shirt":

var query = from p in db.Products
            where p.Name.Contains("t-shirt")
            select p.Name;

For more information on LINQ to SQL, including how to use it to perform common tasks, check out the LINQ to SQL documentation.

