\section{Introduction}

The first order phase transitions (FPT) in QCD at finite temperature and/or baryon chemical potential have been extensively studied in the last two decades. They are characterized by a jump in the pressure, a non-zero susceptibility and a latent heat. The phase transition is usually classified as crossover or FPT based on the behavior of the order parameter, which is usually identified with the chiral condensate for $SU(N)$ QCD with $N_f$ massless quark flavors. In the case of QCD with three light quark flavors, the chiral condensate vanishes at zero baryon chemical potential, and the phase transition is expected to be a crossover at zero chemical potential \cite{Stephanov:1996ki}. However, as the baryon chemical potential is increased, the chiral condensate becomes non-zero and the phase transition is expected to become a FPT \cite{Karsch:2001cy,Stephanov:2004wx}. 

In the context of the Polyakov loop model, the order parameter is usually chosen to be the expectation value of the Polyakov loop. For $SU(3)$ QCD, the phase transition is a crossover at zero baryon chemical potential but a FPT at high enough baryon chemical potential \cite{Bazavov:2017dus}. For $SU(2)$ QCD, the phase transition is a FPT even at low baryon chemical potential \cite{Chen:2018wqf}. 

In this work we study the phase structure of $SU(3)$ QCD with 12 massless quark flavors. This is a theory with an exact global flavor symmetry $SU(12)_L\times SU(12)_R$. The number of quark flavors is large enough that the system is in the conformal window. As a result, the phase transition is expected to be a crossover at zero baryon chemical potential \cite{Kogut:2007zg}. However, as the baryon chemical potential is increased, the global flavor symmetry is spontaneously broken, and the order parameter is no longer the Polyakov loop. The order parameter should now be the expectation value of the Polyakov loop for each of the 12 flavor multiplets. As a result, the phase transition is expected to be a FPT at high enough baryon chemical potential. 

The goal of this paper is to study the phase structure of this theory using lattice simulations. We will use the standard Wilson action for the gauge fields and the staggered fermion action for the quarks. We will use the Polyakov loop and its conjugate as the order parameters. We will study the phase structure at zero temperature and at a fixed temperature. 

We will show that the phase transition is a crossover at zero baryon chemical potential and that it becomes a FPT at high enough baryon chemical potential. We will also show that the transition becomes a FPT earlier than in the case of $SU(3)$ QCD with 3 flavors. This is due to the fact that the quark mass matrix is more degenerate in the case of 12 flavors. The degeneracy is due to the fact that there are many more flavors than colors, and that the quark masses are proportional to the square root of the number of flavors.

The paper is organized as follows. In section \ref{sec:setup} we describe the setup of the lattice simulations. In section \ref{sec:results} we present the results of the simulations. In section \ref{sec:conclusions} we summarize our conclusions. 

\section{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We perform lattice simulations of $SU(3)$ QCD with 12 massless quark flavors. We use the standard Wilson action for the gauge fields and the staggered fermion action for the quarks. The action is given by
\begin{equation}
S = S_g + S_f,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
S_g = -\frac{1}{4} \sum_{x,\mu < \nu} \left(1-\cos\left(\frac{\beta}{2} \sum_{a=1}^3 \epsilon_{abc} G^a_{x,\mu} G^b_{x+\hat{\mu},\nu} \right)\right)
\end{equation}
is the Wilson gauge action and 
\begin{equation}
S_f = \sum_x \bar{\psi}(x) \left( D_4 + m \right) \psi(x)
\end{equation}
is the staggered fermion action, where $D_4$ is the Dirac operator and $m$ is the quark mass. 

We use the standard clover-improved fermions with the coefficient of the improvement term set to $c_{sw}=1.5$. The lattice spacing is set by the Sommer scale $r_0$, which is related to the string tension $\sigma$ by $r_0^2 \sigma = 0.44$. We choose the lattice size to be $16^3 \times 4$ and the lattice spacing to be $a = 0.12 r_0$. The quark mass is set to $m = 0.01 a^{-1}$, which corresponds to a quark mass of $m_q \approx 0.01$ GeV. The physical volume is then $V = (16 r_0)^3 \times 4 r_0 \approx 1.1 \text{ fm}^4$. 

The quark mass matrix is taken to be diagonal with all eigenvalues equal to the quark mass. We consider three different values of the baryon chemical potential: $\mu_B = 0$, $\mu_B = 1.5 T_c$, and $\mu_B = 2.0 T_c$, where $T_c$ is the critical temperature at zero baryon chemical potential. We use the Polyakov loop and its conjugate as the order parameters. The Polyakov loop is defined as 
\begin{equation}
P_i = \frac{1}{3} \sum_{\mu=1}^3 \operatorname{Tr} \left( P_{i,\mu} \right),
\end{equation}
where $P_{i,\mu}$ is the Polyakov line in the $i$th color direction and $\mu$ is the spatial direction. The conjugate of the Polyakov loop is defined as 
\begin{equation}
P_i^\dagger = \frac{1}{3} \sum_{\mu=1}^3 \operatorname{Tr} \left( P_{i,\mu}^\dagger \right).
\end{equation}

