The issue in your code is related to the fact that you are not handling the case where the user inputs an invalid operator. If the user enters an invalid operator, the program will exit the `if-else` block without reading the second number (`y`). This can cause undefined behavior or a crash when the program tries to use `y` later.

To fix this, you should ensure that the program handles invalid operators gracefully by skipping the part where it reads the second number. Here's the corrected version of your code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main() {
    string op;
    double x, y, z;
    cout << "Enter operator (+, -, *, /): ";
    cin >> op;

    // Check if the operator is valid
    if (op == "+" || op == "-" || op == "*" || op == "/") {
        cout << "Enter 1st number: ";
        cin >> x;

        cout << "Enter 2nd number: ";
        cin >> y;

